b/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dec57432-f09d-5d34-90ed-7265e888dca7" :answer/id #uuid "dec57432-f09d-5d34-90ed-7265e888dca7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{60}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-382b07e6-3ec6-5b8a-957e-bbdfe8a69d35" :answer/id #uuid "382b07e6-3ec6-5b8a-957e-bbdfe8a69d35" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{63}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7a62fde9-6a09-5998-9eb3-129787e83051" :question/id #uuid "7a62fde9-6a09-5998-9eb3-129787e83051" :question/math-academy-id "q-223196" :question/problem "![](images/40/409b89d99c494f3273ce228ebb828e440a3c5ab4940344e775680225eed84d8b.png)\n\nGiven the quadrilateral above, we have $x=${{field-1}}\n${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the sum of the exterior angles of a polygon is ${360}^{∘}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+(x+{40}^{∘})+(3x-{40}^{∘})+3x & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 9x & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={40}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f4a24cec-e3bb-5524-a269-d61c5cfa2b21"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f4a24cec-e3bb-5524-a269-d61c5cfa2b21" :answer-field/id #uuid "f4a24cec-e3bb-5524-a269-d61c5cfa2b21" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-a0956c0d-2efe-5ef7-923e-90941b8f2e8b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a0956c0d-2efe-5ef7-923e-90941b8f2e8b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a0956c0d-2efe-5ef7-923e-90941b8f2e8b" :answer/id #uuid "a0956c0d-2efe-5ef7-923e-90941b8f2e8b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "40" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-88db253f-f4d0-5a45-9c21-b523cf1fc7e0" :question/id #uuid "88db253f-f4d0-5a45-9c21-b523cf1fc7e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-223263" :question/problem "![](images/e0/e07e30d981b25aefa18192e38408516423072dcd84e2352bc962df3888c41a04.png)\n\nFor the pentagon above, determine the measure of the exterior angle of $∠A.$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the measure of the exterior angle at $D$ is ${180}^{∘}-(6x+{4}^{∘})={176}^{∘}-6x.$\n\nSince the sum of the exterior angles of a polygon is ${360}^{∘},$  we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(9x-{17}^{∘})+(5x-{5}^{∘})+4x+({176}^{∘}-6x)+(3x+{11}^{∘}) & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ (9x+5x+4x-6x+3x)+(-{17}^{∘}-{5}^{∘}+{176}^{∘}+{11}^{∘}) & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 15x+{165}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 15x & ={195}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={13}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the measure of the exterior angle of $∠A$ equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9x-{17}^{∘} & =9({13}^{∘})-{17}^{∘} \\\\  & ={117}^{∘}-{17}^{∘} \\\\  & ={100}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f63f6de2-8f84-5c1b-88a7-4d31118f7fa1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f63f6de2-8f84-5c1b-88a7-4d31118f7fa1" :answer-field/id #uuid "f63f6de2-8f84-5c1b-88a7-4d31118f7fa1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4b0a8c65-746c-5068-9c13-fbbadc1ed3d2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4b0a8c65-746c-5068-9c13-fbbadc1ed3d2" "answer-a61c3dfc-f5dc-56b4-98d9-a4c8a9519960" "answer-38ad3649-c78e-5b83-b0f1-c3b2b5cc427e" "answer-ac2705c8-1eb8-5d43-ad6c-71362d85e87b" "answer-41a4a9df-6823-5440-9387-1d9555aa339f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4b0a8c65-746c-5068-9c13-fbbadc1ed3d2" :answer/id #uuid "4b0a8c65-746c-5068-9c13-fbbadc1ed3d2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{100}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a61c3dfc-f5dc-56b4-98d9-a4c8a9519960" :answer/id #uuid "a61c3dfc-f5dc-56b4-98d9-a4c8a9519960" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{102}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-38ad3649-c78e-5b83-b0f1-c3b2b5cc427e" :answer/id #uuid "38ad3649-c78e-5b83-b0f1-c3b2b5cc427e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{105}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ac2705c8-1eb8-5d43-ad6c-71362d85e87b" :answer/id #uuid "ac2705c8-1eb8-5d43-ad6c-71362d85e87b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{95}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-41a4a9df-6823-5440-9387-1d9555aa339f" :answer/id #uuid "41a4a9df-6823-5440-9387-1d9555aa339f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{98}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186106488 :question/problem "![](images/ac/ac5d02edc0945574b5bf8f9a1112e9d7e9ca33ebc7714bfadcd14d02e46c43c7.png)\n\nFor the pentagon above, determine the measure of the exterior angle of $∠A.$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the measure of the exterior angle at $C$ is ${180}^{∘}-(4x-{1}^{∘})={181}^{∘}-4x,$ and the measure of the exterior angle at $E$ is ${180}^{∘}-{86}^{∘}={94}^{∘}.$\n\nSince the sum of the exterior angles of a polygon is ${360}^{∘},$  we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x+{1}^{∘})+(2x+{14}^{∘})+({181}^{∘}-4x)+(2x+{16}^{∘})+{94}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ (2x+2x-4x+2x)+({1}^{∘}+{14}^{∘}+{181}^{∘}+{16}^{∘}+{94}^{∘}) & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 2x+{306}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 2x & ={54}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={27}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the measure of the exterior angle of $∠A$ equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+{1}^{∘} & =2({27}^{∘})+{1}^{∘} \\\\  & ={55}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105076 :answer-field/correct "answer-5bbf1ef1-2900-52be-85be-fb6bf9f5cb6e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d4ecbfb9-f75b-5c87-90a2-1055bbd237bc" "answer-9a3c9eb3-21b9-5e17-8373-46790271e175" "answer-04d78c07-0074-5c61-ad9f-15cb9fa4bc7a" "answer-5bbf1ef1-2900-52be-85be-fb6bf9f5cb6e" "answer-2d8179f8-7c80-5afc-b348-1ee744151b97"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d4ecbfb9-f75b-5c87-90a2-1055bbd237bc" :answer/id #uuid "d4ecbfb9-f75b-5c87-90a2-1055bbd237bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{43}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9a3c9eb3-21b9-5e17-8373-46790271e175" :answer/id #uuid "9a3c9eb3-21b9-5e17-8373-46790271e175" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-04d78c07-0074-5c61-ad9f-15cb9fa4bc7a" :answer/id #uuid "04d78c07-0074-5c61-ad9f-15cb9fa4bc7a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{53}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5bbf1ef1-2900-52be-85be-fb6bf9f5cb6e" :answer/id #uuid "5bbf1ef1-2900-52be-85be-fb6bf9f5cb6e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{55}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2d8179f8-7c80-5afc-b348-1ee744151b97" :answer/id #uuid "2d8179f8-7c80-5afc-b348-1ee744151b97" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{65}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186107162 :tutorial/content "Consider the polygon $ABCD$, where the side $\\overline{AB}$ is extended beyond the vertex $B.$\n\n![](images/fa/fa23c036920e2c506c38f1e7be8a498c0a349ef145e8c444201665345bc648e4.png)\n\nWe call $\\color{blue}\\angle{CBE}$ the **exterior angle** of the vertex $B$.\n\nGiven a polygon's vertex, the interior and exterior angles are supplementary. So, for the vertex $B,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\color{YellowOrange}m\\angle{ABC}}_{\\large\\text{Interior}} \\:+\\: \\underbrace{\\color{blue}m\\angle{CBE}}_{\\large\\text{Exterior}} = 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nWe have the following theorem:\n\nThe measures of the exterior angles of any simple polygon sum to $360^\\circ.$\n\nWe'll show why this theorem is true at the end of the lesson.\n\nLet's label the exterior angles of our polygon below:\n\n![](images/ac/acc0ac90f27a78e83b6b14651f61ddfe6a87afa56b6e51a1af57f5e74c31689f.png)\n\nAccording to our theorem, these four angles sum to $360^\\circ.$\n\n$$\na+b+c+d = 360^\\circ\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186107163 :tutorial/content "Why do the exterior angles of a polygon always sum to $360^\\circ?$\n\nSuppose we have an $n$-sided polygon. Then, we have the following:\n\n- There are $n$ pairs of interior-exterior angles, each pair being supplementary (i.e., their measures add to $180^\\circ).$\n\n- Therefore, the sum of the measures of *all* interior-exterior pairs equals ${\\color{blue}n\\cdot 180^\\circ}.$\n\n- In a previous lesson, we saw that the sum of the *interior* angles equals ${\\color{red}{(n-2)\\cdot 180^\\circ}}.$\n\nTherefore, to get the sum of the *exterior* angles, we subtract ${\\color{red}{(n-2)\\cdot 180^\\circ}}$ from ${\\color{blue}n\\cdot 180^\\circ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Sum of Exterior Angles} & =n\\cdot {180}^{∘}-(n-2)\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\  & =n\\cdot {180}^{∘}-n\\cdot {180}^{∘}+2\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\cancel{n\\cdot {180}^{∘}}-\\cancel{n\\cdot {180}^{∘}}+2\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis proves that the sum of the exterior angles of a (simple) polygon equals $360^\\circ.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105975 :activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186478315 :answer-field/correct "answer-d5430906-9808-587e-9c79-133123248909" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d5430906-9808-587e-9c79-133123248909"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d5430906-9808-587e-9c79-133123248909" :answer/id #uuid "d5430906-9808-587e-9c79-133123248909" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value ">" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186084596 :answer-field/choices 17592186084597] [:db/retract 17592186084596 :answer-field/choices 17592186084598] [:db/retract 17592186084596 :answer-field/choices 17592186084599] [:db/retract 17592186084596 :answer-field/choices 17592186084600] [:db/retract 17592186084596 :answer-field/choices 17592186084601] [:db/retract 17592186084602 :answer-field/choices 17592186084603] [:db/retract 17592186084602 :answer-field/choices 17592186084604] [:db/retract 17592186084602 :answer-field/choices 17592186084605] [:db/retract 17592186084602 :answer-field/choices 17592186084606] [:db/retract 17592186084602 :answer-field/choices 17592186084607] {:db/id 17592186087835 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-38e098ba-2563-5c51-8af3-272acb29f55b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087836 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8c404e97-7fab-5c9d-b54c-fbf58817ca24"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087838 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c693f3f3-9251-58ce-8006-d594413ed6b7" "question-48e55e9e-f8d4-5cc0-b60c-cb504ca3f4b2"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-38e098ba-2563-5c51-8af3-272acb29f55b" :question/id #uuid "38e098ba-2563-5c51-8af3-272acb29f55b" :question/math-academy-id "q-277800" :question/problem "From **left to right,** the $x$-intercepts of the hyperbola $9{x}^{2}-16{y}^{2}=144$ are\n\n$($\n{{field-1}}, \n{{field-2}} $)\\,$\nand \n$\\,($\n{{field-3}}, \n{{field-4}} $).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the $x$-intercepts of the hyperbola, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}-16{y}^{2} & =144 \\\\ 9{x}^{2}-16(0)^{2} & =144 \\\\ 9{x}^{2} & =144 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =16 \\\\ x & =\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the $x$-intercepts are $(-4,0)$ and $(4,0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1fcf8298-71f1-5277-a670-52f4b959a58d" "answer-field-8accc043-d509-5f60-b527-27cf0313ff31" "answer-field-8aff0d3f-cbbf-51f8-bbc5-35a4ffbdea23" "answer-field-dba14e00-f776-528f-9d2d-03dadc679c80"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1fcf8298-71f1-5277-a670-52f4b959a58d" :answer-field/id #uuid "1fcf8298-71f1-5277-a670-52f4b959a58d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-872ca58d-51f5-5476-bd9b-c65086ba14e3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-872ca58d-51f5-5476-bd9b-c65086ba14e3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-872ca58d-51f5-5476-bd9b-c65086ba14e3" :answer/id #uuid "872ca58d-51f5-5476-bd9b-c65086ba14e3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8accc043-d509-5f60-b527-27cf0313ff31" :answer-field/id #uuid "8accc043-d509-5f60-b527-27cf0313ff31" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-6eb336b9-e623-5013-affb-62dd0c5e6e7d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6eb336b9-e623-5013-affb-62dd0c5e6e7d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6eb336b9-e623-5013-affb-62dd0c5e6e7d" :answer/id #uuid "6eb336b9-e623-5013-affb-62dd0c5e6e7d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8aff0d3f-cbbf-51f8-bbc5-35a4ffbdea23" :answer-field/id #uuid "8aff0d3f-cbbf-51f8-bbc5-35a4ffbdea23" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-1c6f2b10-03a3-5d5f-9676-fb1ab79776ff" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1c6f2b10-03a3-5d5f-9676-fb1ab79776ff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1c6f2b10-03a3-5d5f-9676-fb1ab79776ff" :answer/id #uuid "1c6f2b10-03a3-5d5f-9676-fb1ab79776ff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-dba14e00-f776-528f-9d2d-03dadc679c80" :answer-field/id #uuid "dba14e00-f776-528f-9d2d-03dadc679c80" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-7ddee74d-5c9a-50c7-8819-1059124f4de2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7ddee74d-5c9a-50c7-8819-1059124f4de2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ddee74d-5c9a-50c7-8819-1059124f4de2" :answer/id #uuid "7ddee74d-5c9a-50c7-8819-1059124f4de2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087977 :question/problem "Calculate the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ y & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercepts are $(0,\\pm 2).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186084596 :answer-field/correct "answer-75c21824-a155-50ad-8c06-d0ffc9cfcfad" :answer-field/choices ["answer-75c21824-a155-50ad-8c06-d0ffc9cfcfad" "answer-acf64965-e02c-505f-870b-4d186b665498" "answer-6d3ff4c2-b1b9-5057-be7e-101bc3d7ddcb" "answer-73b25063-1e07-516a-8a8e-3b366b4d0aa1" "answer-1e2b5f68-4936-51d3-a8b2-ec31db3c12c6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-75c21824-a155-50ad-8c06-d0ffc9cfcfad" :answer/id #uuid "75c21824-a155-50ad-8c06-d0ffc9cfcfad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0,\\pm 2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-acf64965-e02c-505f-870b-4d186b665498" :answer/id #uuid "acf64965-e02c-505f-870b-4d186b665498" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0,\\pm 4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6d3ff4c2-b1b9-5057-be7e-101bc3d7ddcb" :answer/id #uuid "6d3ff4c2-b1b9-5057-be7e-101bc3d7ddcb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0,\\pm 9)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-73b25063-1e07-516a-8a8e-3b366b4d0aa1" :answer/id #uuid "73b25063-1e07-516a-8a8e-3b366b4d0aa1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(0,-2)$ and $(0,3)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1e2b5f68-4936-51d3-a8b2-ec31db3c12c6" :answer/id #uuid "1e2b5f68-4936-51d3-a8b2-ec31db3c12c6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(0,-3)$ and $(0,2)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8c404e97-7fab-5c9d-b54c-fbf58817ca24" :question/id #uuid "8c404e97-7fab-5c9d-b54c-fbf58817ca24" :question/math-academy-id "q-278361" :question/problem "From **top to bottom,** the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}=1$ are\n\n$($\n{{field-1}}, \n{{field-2}} $)\\,$\nand\n$\\,($\n{{field-3}}, \n{{field-4}} $).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =16 \\\\ y & =\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, from top to bottom, the $y$-intercepts are $(0,4)$ and $(0,-4).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7a36e933-a342-57e4-aa8d-681fff44a1c9" "answer-field-eb80c4c5-d0ec-59e7-9b08-3cbc075065a4" "answer-field-bba18ebf-7918-5ca8-98f9-2a0e8cb3d1bd" "answer-field-a7e90dbf-04dd-5b01-8f52-5588cf1af79c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7a36e933-a342-57e4-aa8d-681fff44a1c9" :answer-field/id #uuid "7a36e933-a342-57e4-aa8d-681fff44a1c9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-32c32583-0807-50cf-a7d2-93b5221acc41" :answer-field/choices ["answer-32c32583-0807-50cf-a7d2-93b5221acc41"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-32c32583-0807-50cf-a7d2-93b5221acc41" :answer/id #uuid "32c32583-0807-50cf-a7d2-93b5221acc41" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-eb80c4c5-d0ec-59e7-9b08-3cbc075065a4" :answer-field/id #uuid "eb80c4c5-d0ec-59e7-9b08-3cbc075065a4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-e1fb69b5-5fdf-5054-908c-8af0d398b75e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e1fb69b5-5fdf-5054-908c-8af0d398b75e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e1fb69b5-5fdf-5054-908c-8af0d398b75e" :answer/id #uuid "e1fb69b5-5fdf-5054-908c-8af0d398b75